Envelope parsing (long header):
```
p = pos
if (p >= wire.length) break;  // can't happen since pos < len
int first = wire[p] & 0xff;
if ((first & 0x80) != 0) {  // long header
   if ((first & 0x40) == 0) → BAD_HEADER (fixed bit)
   if ((first & 0x0c) != 0) → BAD_HEADER (reserved)
   int typeBits = (first & 0x30) >> 4;
   if (typeBits == 0) level = I; else if (typeBits == 2) level = H; else BAD_HEADER
   int pnWidth = (first & 0x03) + 1;
   p++;
   need 4 bytes version == 00000001 else BAD_HEADER
   p += 4
   need 1 byte dcidLen; if dcidLen > 20 → BAD_HEADER; need dcidLen bytes
   need 1 byte scidLen; if scidLen > 20 → BAD_HEADER; need scidLen bytes
   if level == I: read varint tokenLen (need enough bytes); need tokenLen bytes → token
   read varint length (need enough bytes) → len
   if (len < pnWidth) BAD_HEADER
   if (len > wire.length - p) BAD_HEADER
   pn bytes = wire[p .. p+pnWidth); payload = wire[p+pnWidth .. p+len)
   nextPos = p + len
}
```
Note: "Overlong header/body integers are allowed" — varints may use up to 8 bytes. tokenLen/len values up to 2^62-1; if len > remaining → BAD_HEADER. Careful with len as a long: `len > wire.length - p` comparison — since wire.length ≤ 8 MiB, any len > 2^31 is definitely too big. Use long comparison.

Varint reading in the header: if there aren't enough bytes for the varint (e.g., first byte says 8-byte width but only 3 remain) → BAD_HEADER (truncated header).

Short header:
```
first = wire[p] & 0xff
if ((first & 0x80) != 0) → long (handled above)
if ((first & 0x40) == 0) → BAD_HEADER (fixed bit must be 1)
if ((first & 0x18) != 0) → BAD_HEADER (reserved bits 4,3 must be 0)
int phase = (first >> 2) & 1;
int pnWidth = (first & 3) + 1;
p++;
dcid = wire[p .. p+localCidWidth) — need enough bytes else BAD_HEADER
pn = wire[p .. p+pnWidth) — need enough else BAD_HEADER
payload = rest of datagram
nextPos = wire.length (terminal)
```

